  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nThe strong pressure gradient and resultant strong easterly winds \nacross southern AZ will weaken heading into Sunday as the strong \nsurface high in the Plains weakens and shifts to the Mid-Atlantic.\nWinds early Sunday morning will remain elevated over south-\ncentral Arizona and the higher terrain with gusts between 20-25 in\nSC AZ, and 30-35 mph in the higher terrain. Past sunrise these \nwinds will quickly weaken as the upper level pattern continues to \nquickly shift. The ridge will continue to push eastwards, and the \ncutoff low will begin to weaken and become an open wave, lifting \nthrough the region Sunday- Monday. As the open wave moves drifts \nthrough, lingering light showers/virga remain in the forecast, \n20-35% Sunday, then 10-20% Monday. This will be confined to the \nhigher terrain areas, where with enough extra lift may be possible\nfor showers to develop. However, if any activity does develop \ntotal accumulations are expected to be only a few hundredths, or \nnear zero. For the lower deserts temperatures are expected to \nrebound back into the mid to upper 80s Sunday and into early next \nweek.\n\nBy late Monday into Tuesday a troughing feature will begin to dive \ndown the western coastline, however confidence is low in the timing \nand strength of the feature. This has resulted in a decent amount of \nspread in for temperatures by mid next week. As it stands now temperatures\nwill remain near to slightly below normal Monday-Tuesday then start\nto fall 6-9 degrees below normal for the later half of the week.",
